BUILDING
========

Odin is built on GNU/Linux with GCC (C++20), CMake 3.20+ and pkg-config.
It needs SDL 3, GLEW, Jansson, the Vulkan headers, glslangValidator and an
OpenGL 4.3 driver.

 $ cmake -B build
 $ cmake --build build -j

The binary is build/bin/odin, with its data in build/share/odin. To install
(default prefix /usr/local):

 $ cmake -B build -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$HOME/.local
 $ cmake --build build -j && cmake --install build

Options (-D<option>=<value>):

 ODIN_NATIVE=OFF	no -march=native, for packages and cross builds
 ODIN_LTO=OFF		no -flto
 ODIN_OPT_FLAGS=<flags>	default "-O3 -fomit-frame-pointer -fno-stack-protector"
 ODIN_PGO=generate|use	profile-guided build
 ODIN_VERSION=<commit>	version string when building without git
 GLSLANG=<path>		glslangValidator to use
 ODIN_TESTS=ON		build the tests
 ODIN_GLES=ON		OpenGL ES 3.1+ instead of OpenGL (Linux)

OpenGL ES 3.1 needs GL_EXT_geometry_shader for translucent polygons.

Leave CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE empty: Release adds -DNDEBUG, which drops asserts.

Profile-guided build (4-15 % faster):

 $ cmake -B build -DODIN_PGO=generate && cmake --build build -j
 $ build/bin/odin ...
 $ cmake -B build -DODIN_PGO=use && cmake --build build -j

Cross build:

 $ cmake -B build -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=<toolchain.cmake> -DODIN_NATIVE=OFF \
       -DGLSLANG=<host>/bin/glslangValidator -DODIN_VERSION=<commit>
 $ cmake --build build -j
 $ DESTDIR=<target> cmake --install build --prefix /usr

Tests and the layer check:

 $ cmake -B build -DODIN_TESTS=ON && cmake --build build -j
 $ ctest --test-dir build
 $ cmake --build build --target check-layers

On macOS, with the Xcode Command Line Tools and Homebrew:

 $ brew install cmake pkgconf sdl3 glew jansson vulkan-headers vulkan-loader \
       molten-vk glslang
 $ cmake -B build && cmake --build build -j

Vulkan draws there by default, through MoltenVK. OpenGL stops at 4.1 on
macOS: with api = opengl Odin draws with it all the same, its translucent
fragments peeled one layer at a time instead of put in lists (the same
pictures, slower where they pile up).


RUNNING
=======

 $ build/bin/odin -R <ROM directory> -r <game>

(-R may be left out once the menu, page Paths, keeps the folder: roms in
[paths] of odin.ini.) Without -r, as a double click starts it, Odin opens
its library: the games of the list, those whose files are missing said so
with the files and the sets of MAME that hold them, and buttons to choose
the folders of the games and of the BIOS (kept in [paths]); the arrows, the
D-pad or the stick of a gamepad, or a click choose a game, Enter, A of the
gamepad (as [menu] press says) or a second click plays it, and when it ends
(but by Ctrl+Q, which quits) the library comes back, as it does after Close
game of the bar from any game. The bar is over the library at all times,
and the menu (Escape, F10 or Guide) opens there as in a game, its page Input
on the game chosen. The pictures of the games
are in the folder of the artwork (artwork/ in the data folder of Odin,
beside saves/; artwork in [paths], page Paths; ODIN_ARTWORK=<folder> another
one, none none), named after the game in MAME, in any case:
<game>_title.png, the title Odin keeps the first time the game shows it with
no key or button pressed since its power-on, is its picture in the library;
<game>_logo.png, a picture of the user, is shown there instead;
<game>_bezel.png is laid over the window where it is wider than the screen
of the game, the game in its hole: the part not opaque joined to its middle
(the glass of a screen often is half so), or as the <game>_bezel.info of
Batocera beside it says (swracer_logo.png, swracer_bezel.png). bezel in
[display] (page Display) says what is around the game there: off, black;
blur, the light of its edges, softer and dimmer away from it; image, its
bezel, black without one. The games are those of odin-games.json, by the
names of their MAME sets: airtrix, braveff, pharrier, sgnascar, swracer and
von4. The file is looked for in the current directory, then in $HOME, then
in ~/.local/share/odin.
The ROM directory holds each game as MAME keeps it: a directory, a zip or a
7z of the name of the game, with the files odin-games.json lists for it. The
files a game shares with others, in the sets of MAME hikaru (the BIOS), mie,
jvs13551 and 18wheelr, are looked for in the BIOS directory (-B <path>, bios
in [paths], page Paths), then in the ROM directory: hikaru.zip, hikaru.7z or
a directory hikaru; they may be in the directory or the archive of the game
too. A file is the one of its size and of the CRC MAME gives it: in an
archive by its CRC, whatever its name; in a directory by its name, with or
without dashes, in any case, else by its CRC. One found by its name with
another CRC is said, and taken (another dump of the chip). When a file is
missing, Odin says which, and the set of MAME that holds it. The directories
may have names in any language (on Windows, 10 version 1903 or later); the
menu writes them with its own font, Cozette, drawn by pixels (Latin, Greek,
Cyrillic, Vietnamese), its letters as large as whole pixels allow, and the
other letters with the fonts the system has: on Windows those it comes
with, on Linux DejaVu Sans and, for Chinese, Japanese and Korean, Noto Sans
CJK, Droid Sans Fallback or WenQuanYi, on macOS Arial Unicode, Hiragino
Sans GB and Apple SD Gothic Neo. The programs of the I/O boards of
SWRACER (315-6215.bin) and NASCAR (epr-21868.ic7, from the 18wheelr set of
MAME) may be left out: an imitation of the board answers then, made from
that program and checked against it. The games play the same without them,
and NASCAR sends its wheel (the force feedback) the same orders; with them
the board runs its own program, a little slower. So does the program of the
drive board of NASCAR, the board that drives the motor of its wheel
(epr-20512.bin, from the srally2 set of MAME): with it, a wheel with force
feedback turns as that board tells its motor, the board starting with the
game (about 11 s, when it turns the wheel back to its centre by itself, as
on the cabinet) and answering it; without it (or with ODIN_DRIVE=imitation),
the wheel takes the force of each order of the game at once.

Options:

 -n <frame>		stops after that frame
 -s <frame>[,...]	saves <game>-<frame>.odinstate after these frames
 -l <file>		loads a state at startup
 -u			lifts the limit of 60 frames per second
 -x <num>		draws the 3D num times finer than 640 by 480 (1 to 6)
 -a <num>		filters the textures with an anisotropy of num (2 to
			16); the board has none
 -w <num>		makes the window num times 640 by 480
 -f			takes the whole display (F11 or Alt+Enter goes back
			and forth)
 -v			says each access of the board (slow; also "verbose = on"
			in [log] of odin.ini, page Paths of the menu)

Keys: Escape or F10 opens the menu, which pauses the game (its Display
page sets the window and the picture, kept in [display] of odin.ini:
scale = 1 to 6, upscale_mode = raw, snap or native (see below),
anisotropy = 1 to 16, window = 1 to 6 or a size, 1600x900
(Custom: the window resized by hand keeps its size for the next start),
vsync = off, on or adaptive (the frames on time wait for the display, a
late one does not), fullscreen, unthrottled and show_speed = on or off,
api = opengl or vulkan (what draws, from the next start of Odin; Vulkan
1.3, or 1.1 with the same as extensions, else OpenGL draws; OpenGL 4.3,
else 4.1 as on macOS; vulkan by default on macOS, opengl elsewhere);
the options say the same and come first; no vsync at an unlimited pace,
nor vsync = on on a display slower than 60 frames a second),
Ctrl+Q quits, P pauses, F4 saves <game>.odinstate and F1 loads it, in
~/.local/share/odin/states (states in [paths] of odin.ini, page Paths),
Ctrl+R resets (the cabinet switched off and on), F11 or Alt+Enter takes
the whole display, F6 puts the card of Virtual-On in or takes it out (see
below). The page Hotkeys of the menu changes these keys and the buttons
below, kept in [hotkeys] of odin.ini:

 [hotkeys]
 pause = Pause, pad:guide+x

(menu, pause, save_state, load_state, reset, fullscreen, card and quit: two keys
at most, Ctrl+Q written so, and a button held with Guide,
pad:guide+<button>, or pad:guide for Guide alone; none for nothing.)

The picture can go through a CRT filter, off by default: page Display,
FILTER, or in [display] of odin.ini

 [display]
 filter = lola-retro/sega-monitor-29

none, lola-retro/sega-monitor-29 (the 29-inch 31 kHz monitor of Planet
Harriers and NASCAR Arcade) or lola-retro/sega-projector-50 (the 50-inch
31 kHz projector of the other deluxe cabinets), after Sega's manuals, on
the shaders of Lola Retro (by Zoki, MIT); or one of Lola's own monitors,
lola-retro/pc-monitor-vga and its -good, -premium, -premium-plus,
-extreme and -flat. It draws the 640 x 480 picture of the board as the
tube shows it and asks much of the graphics card (a weak one cannot keep
60 frames a second). Its files are in filters/, where odin-games.json is
(with Vulkan, the SPIR-V the build leaves beside its shaders too).

At a scale above 1, the pieces of the picture flat to the screen (the HUD,
its text) can be drawn as the 640 x 480 screen of the board has them: page
Display, Upscaling mode, or upscale_mode in [display]. raw, by default,
draws them finer as the rest: thin lines may show between pieces the
games leave a fraction of a pixel apart (around the timer of Virtual-On);
snap puts their corners on the pixels of that screen, their slanted edges
smooth; native has them cover the pixels they cover there, their textures
finer, their edges as on the cabinet (with Vulkan, on a card with geometry
shaders; snap on another). Both may split a piece made of a flat part and
a tilted one (the lock-on of Planet Harriers).

In a game, the mouse moved over the window shows the bar at its top a
while, the game going on under it; in a game the mouse aims in (Brave Fire
Fighters), only from the top edge of the window. Its lists set what the
pages of the menu set, without opening it. FILE: Open game... plays the
zip or the 7z of a game, the game of its name in MAME, from its directory;
Close game goes back to the library, Reset switches the cabinet off and
on; the state of the game saved and loaded, the menu (Settings...), Quit.
INPUT, for the game that runs: the gamepad of each player, the dead zone
and the trigger threshold of that gamepad, its inputs restored to their
defaults or cleared, kind of device by kind (Undo a few seconds), the
light gun of each player and the vibration where the game has them; the
hotkeys restored to their defaults, sending the outputs. DISPLAY: the
whole display, the window size, the internal resolution and the upscaling
mode, the anisotropic filtering, the vertical sync, the bezel, the CRT
screen, the speed limit (at the next launch), the FPS in the title, the
menu theme. AUDIO: the volume, the sound in the background, the device,
and how the game that runs plays its optional sound board. LINK: the
network board, on or off, this cabinet and its port (at the next game).
DEBUG pauses the game and turns the detailed log on (at the next launch).
ABOUT opens the page About. The keys of each control and hotkey, the
folders, the address of the next cabinet and a window of a size given by
hand are set in the menu. What the bar changes is kept in odin.ini as the
menu keeps it. What Odin does or cannot do (a state saved, the card taken
in) is said a few seconds in the bottom-left corner, bar or not. In the
library, Close game, Reset, the states, Pause and the players of INPUT are
greyed out.

0 is TEST and 9 SERVICE, the buttons of the cabinet: they open and work
the menu of the BOOTROM and the test menus of the games (both together
leave INPUT TEST); 5 and 6 are the coins. Player 1
has 1 for Start, the arrows, and Left Ctrl, Left Alt, Left Shift, Z, X, C;
player 2 has 2 for Start, R F D G, and A, S, Q, W, E, T. The arrows also
move a wheel, a stick or a sight, a step each frame, and back when let go;
Up is the gas and Down the brake; the second stick of player 1 (ROLL of
Air Trix, the right lever of Racer) is on R F D G. X and C shift the gears
down and up. Virtual-On has the keys of MAME: E S D F the left lever, I J
K L the right one, Left Ctrl and Left Alt the shot and the dash of the
left lever, Space and Left Shift those of the right one.

In Brave the pad of a player shakes while the nozzle of the cabinet does
(Vibration in the advanced options of the page Input, or "vibration =
off" in [players.braveff], stops it).
The lamps and the other outputs of the cabinets go to other programs (a
lamp or a force feedback program) as MAME gives them with its network
output, when odin.ini says so (the menu, page Outputs, turns it on and
off, and shows the outputs of each game light):

 [outputs]
 network = on

(port 8000 of this computer only: "mame_start = <game>", then "<id> = 1"
or "<id> = 0" as each output of the game changes, its id in
odin-games.json, and "mame_stop = 1" at the end.)

Virtual-On Force, NASCAR, Star Wars Racer Arcade and Planet Harriers link
cabinets with the network board 837-13404. Each cabinet is an Odin; the
optical cable of each goes to the next cabinet, the one of the last to the
first, through the network of the computers (the menu, page Link, or
odin.ini):

 [network]
 board = ring
 port = 13404
 next = 192.168.1.20
 cabinet = 1

board: none (no board, as by default), loop (the cable looped back on the
same cabinet), ring (linked to other Odins). port: where the cabinet
before comes in; two Odins of one computer each take their own (13404,
13405...). next: the address of the cabinet after, ":port" after it when
it is not 13404; empty, this cabinet again. cabinet: which cabinet of the
link this one is, 1 to 8, each its own. Odin sets the game as the BIOS
menu and the test menu of the game would: cabinet 1 MASTER, the others
SLAVE (SYSTEM ASSIGNMENTS, COMMUNICATION MODE), and the seat of the
cabinet (Virtual-On A, B...; Racer and NASCAR 1, 2...); a game that has
never run on that computer starts once more to take its seat, and with
board = none it goes back to NO LINK. Two Odins of one computer each have
their own odin.ini, so their own saves: each a portable copy in its own
folder (portable.txt beside it, see below), or ODIN_CONFIG=<file> before
odin, its [paths] saves another folder. Virtual-On in U.S.A. links 2 cabinets
at most. The program of the board needs a frame round the ring in less
than about 15 ms: a network by cable, not by radio, and no further than
the local network.

NASCAR and Planet Harriers have a second sound board for speakers of
their own (the backrest of the seat). Odin plays the two boards on two
speakers, the second one at the level odin-games.json gives it
(option_level, in dB), as the volumes of the cabinet set it; odin.ini
turns that knob, in the section of the game:

 [sound.sgnascar]
 option_level = -15

(-60 to 12 dB; -o quad plays the four channels apart, -o main and
-o option one board alone; "mode = quad" or "mode = main" in that section
too.) The menu, page Audio, sets them, and in [sound] the device the sound
plays on, the volume of Odin and its sound while the window is in the
background:

 [sound]
 device = <a name the page lists>
 volume = 80
 background = off
 In Racer the second board feeds the vibration
device of the control panel, not speakers: the pad of player 1 shakes as
hard as its sound; the page Audio plays it in the speakers too, where the
user wants it ("mode = mix", -13 dB by default, or "mode = quad" in
[sound.swracer]). In NASCAR the pad of player 1 shakes with the shocks
the game gives the wheel, not with the hold and the damping that follow
the moves of the wheel (experimental; Vibration in the advanced options
of the page Input, or "vibration = off" in [players.swracer] or
[players.sgnascar], stops it).

The mouse aims for player 1 in Brave: the sight where it points on the
picture, water on the left button, fog on the right one, Start on the
middle one; off the picture, the nozzle points away from the screen. Of
the keys, a stick and the mouse, the last that moved works a control. The
sight meets the pointer once NOZZLE SENSOR SETTING (in the test menu of
the game) has been done with the mouse.

A light gun (or a second mouse) for each player, read on its own (evdev
on Linux: the user in the group input; Raw Input on Windows), chosen on
the page Input under the device of the player (Light gun), or:

 [players.braveff]
 gun1 = vidpid:1209:0001 GUN4IR
 gun2 = uniq:A1B2C3D4E5 Sinden Lightgun

its identity as Odin's log says it ("pointer: <name> = <node> (aims,
<identity>)"): its serial, else VID:PID, else its port. It aims as the
mouse does, with its buttons, for its player alone; player 1 without one
keeps the mouse. On Linux the guns chosen stop moving the pointer of the
desktop while the game runs, and move it again in the menu.
ODIN_CONTROLLERS=none leaves them out too.

A wheel, its pedals or another joystick that is no gamepad (a flight
stick): one for each player, in the order they are plugged in, or the one
of "joy1 = <GUID> <name>" (joy2: player 2) in [players.<game>]. Odin's log
names each one ("joystick: '<name>' plugged, 4 axes, 25 buttons, 1
hats"). It is read all of its way, without a dead zone, by the numbers
SDL gives its inputs, those EmulationStation gives them on Batocera:
joy:axis0 an axis (the first one steers by default: that of a wheel),
joy:axis2:invert an axis turned over (a pedal at rest at the top of its
axis), joy:-axis1 and joy:+axis1 its halves, joy:button4 a button,
joy:hat0_up a way of a hat. The page Input lists them with the gamepads
(a wheel first in a game with one): a click on a control there, then the
pedal pressed or the button, takes it. The pedals and the buttons change
from one wheel to another; for a Logitech G29 on Linux:

 [controls.sgnascar]
 gas_pedal = key:Up, pad:righttrigger, joy:axis2:invert
 brake_pedal = key:Down, pad:lefttrigger, joy:axis3:invert
 gear_up = key:C, pad:rightshoulder, joy:button4
 gear_down = key:X, pad:leftshoulder, joy:button5

The wheel stays with the driver of the system: SDL's own driver of the
Logitech wheels, which would turn their centring spring off, is off
unless SDL_JOYSTICK_HIDAPI_LG4FF=1 is in the environment.

In NASCAR the wheel of player 1, when it has force feedback, turns as the
game tells the drive board of the cabinet (see the programs above): on
Linux through its event node (the user in the group input, or the rules of
the system that let joysticks be written to), elsewhere through SDL. While
the game runs, the wheel's own centring spring is off and its gain at its
highest, as SDL leaves them; the force lets go when the menu opens, and
stops with the vibration of the page Input ("vibration = off" in
[players.sgnascar]).

Gamepads: one for each player, in the order they are plugged in, or the
one chosen for the game in the menu (Input). SELECT (SHARE) puts in a
coin for the player of the pad. Guide alone opens the menu; at its foot,
Close game (up from its first row), pressed twice, goes back to the
library, as the mouse and the keyboard can there too. Guide held
with Back pauses, with RB saves the state, with LB loads it, with Start
quits (a button pressed with Guide is not the game's), with the left stick
pressed is SERVICE, with the right one TEST. Start, the D-pad, A, B,
X, Y, LB, RB, LT, RT, the sticks and the triggers work the controls of
each game as their roles tell (odin-games.json): for NASCAR,
the wheel on the left stick, the pedals on the triggers, the gears on LB
and RB, the view change on Y (or Z); for Brave, the sight where the left
stick points, the water on RT and the fog on LT; for Planet Harriers,
BULLET on RT, MISSILE on LT and BOMB on A; for Racer, BRAKE on RT and
BOOST on A. Every control of a game can take other keys and pad inputs
in odin.ini, beside the saves (see below), in the section of that game,
under the id odin-games.json gives it:

 [controls.sgnascar]
 view_change = key:Space, pad:b
 gear_up = pad:a
 coin1 = key:5

 [controls.pharrier]
 stick_v = pad:lefty:invert
 stick_h_left = key:A
 stick_h_right = key:D

(pad:-lefty is the left stick up, pad:+leftx the stick to the right,
mouse:left, mouse:middle, mouse:right its buttons, mouse:x and mouse:y
where it points, mouse:off away from the picture;
pad:lefty:invert the whole axis turned over: Planet Harriers then goes up
when the stick goes up, where its cabinet stick, a flight stick, goes up
when pulled.) An analog control takes the axes of a pad on its line, and
keys and buttons on the halves of its way: <id>_left and <id>_right, or
<id>_up and <id>_down; a pedal takes them on its own line, with its axes
(gas_pedal = pad:righttrigger, key:Up).

(pad:guide+leftstick is a button held with Guide.) The menu (Escape, F10
or Guide) writes these lines itself, and the gamepad chosen for a player:

 [players.braveff]
 p1 = 030000005e0400008e02000014010000 Xbox 360 Controller

and for the gamepads the dead zone of their sticks, in % of the way (a
circle round the centre where a stick gives nothing; past it the way
starts from 0), and how far a trigger goes before it presses a button
(the advanced options of the page Input; 0 to 30 and 0 to 50):

 [gamepads]
 dead_zone = 3
 trigger_threshold = 3

a make and model its own in [gamepad.<GUID>], the GUID of its line in
[players.<game>]:

 [gamepad.030000005e0400008e02000014010000]
 dead_zone = 8

and the controls of the page Input as a list rather than on the drawing
(the button List):

 [menu]
 classic = on

(animations = off: in a small window the row of the games, a name
between dots, changes at once rather than moving; theme = runasteinn,
edda, raf or heimdall: the colours of the menu, the bar and the library,
page Display.)

In the menu, LB and RB of a gamepad change the page, LT and RT the game
(on the page Input they are tested as the controls they work: there the
first two rows of the frame, the tabs and the games, change them), B goes
back (a list dropped open closes first, then the options, a key caught is
let go, else the game goes on). The D-pad or the left stick moves a frame
from row to row; left and right change a choice or a slider, A presses. On
the page Input the frame goes from control to control: A changes it, X
clears it and Y sets it back to its default, on the device shown; during
Map all, A held a second skips a control (let go sooner, it is the button
the control takes). [menu] gives them other inputs, as [controls.<game>]
does: up, down, left, right, press, back, clear, default, page_before,
page_after, game_before, game_after.

Odin writes every key it knows in odin.ini, at the start, those at their
value by default as comments ("# volume = 100"): taking the # away and
changing the value changes it. The menu writes a change in place, and
the comment again for a value set back to its default.

The cabinet has 1p_service and 2p_service, coin1 and coin2, test (of the
I/O board), board_test and board_service; a game without controls in the
list has 1p_start, 1p_up, 1p_down, 1p_left, 1p_right, 1p_push1 to 1p_push6,
and the same for 2p. The keys and pad inputs it starts with are in
src/odin/host/input/bindings.cpp; odin-games.json gives a few buttons their own keys
("keys") and pad inputs ("pad").

What the board keeps without power (the settings of the BIOS, the records
and settings of the game, the clock) is read at start and written on exit,
in a folder for each game: ~/.local/share/odin/saves/<game> (under
XDG_DATA_HOME when it is set), %APPDATA%\odin\saves\<game> on Windows,
~/Library/Application Support/odin/saves/<game> on macOS, or the folder
saves in [paths] of odin.ini (page Paths). ODIN_SAVES=<folder> takes
another folder before it, ODIN_SAVES=none keeps nothing. The files have the names
MAME gives them: mie_eeprom (the SYSTEM MENU of the BIOS: country, coins,
cabinet type, sound of the attract), nvram (the game), main_eeprom and
rombd_eeprom (the serial number of the main board, the security of the ROM
board). A game without mie_eeprom starts as a new U.S.A. cabinet left the
factory: the settings of its manual. Odin calibrates no control in
advance: the board of Air Trix steers once it is calibrated in the test
menu of the game (CALIBRATION), with the controller you play with, and
the other games have their own pages for it. The settings of Odin are in
odin.ini in the same odin folder; ODIN_CONFIG=<file> reads another one.

The card of Virtual-On goes in and out as at the cabinet, by the player:
nothing goes in by itself. The cards are kept with the saves of the game,
in its folder cards (<name>.card); the menu FILE > Card in hand chooses
the one in hand ([players.von4] card = <name>). When the game asks for a
card (choosing the robot), F6 or FILE > Insert card puts it into the slot;
at the end of the game the reader writes it and ejects it, and the game
waits until the player takes it back, F6 or FILE > Remove card. A new card
comes from the cabinet set as a terminal (the test menu of the game, GAME
ASSIGNMENTS, GAME START MODE = VENDOR, with FREE PLAY): taken back, it is
kept as card-<n>. Faster, FILE > New card... makes the card that terminal
makes for a new pilot of the name typed (up to 8 characters of its
keyboard: A to Z, 0 to 9, space and @,./~_:;^!?<>+-*=%'"()), on the first
VR of its list, and puts it in hand as <name>.card (a number after the
name when that file is there already). A note says when the reader takes
the card in and when a card comes out at the slot. A card file is written
whole or not at all, the card it held before kept beside it as
<name>.card.bak, which Odin reads in its place should the card file be cut
short. Virtual-On reads cards in the country Japan only (the SYSTEM MENU
of the BIOS); elsewhere it never asks for one.

A portable Odin keeps its files in its own folder: put an empty file
portable.txt beside the program (odin.exe, or the AppImage), or its
odin.ini. Its odin.ini, saves, states, odin.log and the cache of its
shaders are then there, apart from those of any other copy; it says
"portable:" and the folder at the start. The variables above still come
first.

What Odin says (in the terminal it was started from; on Windows, opened by
a double click, it has none) is written to odin.log in the same odin folder
too, from scratch at each launch; the log of the launch before is kept as
odin-previous.log.
ODIN_LOG=<file> writes another file, ODIN_LOG=none none. The file stops at
64 MB (the detailed log of -v says a lot); after a crash it holds the last
words of Odin.

The comments at the top of the source files tell the other variables of the
environment; they are for debugging.
